/** \addtogroup area_distributed */
/** \defgroup theory_distributed Theory of distributed algorithms
 \ingroup area_distributed

 \details The headers of `include/ADS/distributed` realize, beyond the book,
 the algorithms whose data are spread over the processes of MPI, each with a
 memory of its own, which exchange them only by messages; the area holds the
 sort by regular sampling of `DistributedSort.h`, whose processes may also
 run on the threads of an executor (see \ref theory_parallel). This page
 collects what the header takes for granted: the model of the cost of the
 messages and its notation (see \ref theory_distributed_notation), and the
 property on which the sort rests, with the argument behind its bound (see
 \ref theory_distributed_sort). The pseudocode of the sort is instead on the
 page of its header, in the comment of its function, together with the paper
 that it follows.

 \tableofcontents

 \section theory_distributed_notation Definitions and notation

 A distributed computation runs on \f$ P \f$ processes, numbered by their
 <em>ranks</em> \f$ 0 , \ldots , P - 1 \f$ in a communicator, each with its
 own memory and its own part of the input; a process reads the data of
 another only by receiving a message. A <em>collective</em> operation is one
 that all the processes of the communicator call, each with its part, e.g.,
 the gathering of a value of each process in all of them
 (`MPI_Allgather`) or the exchange in which each process sends a piece of its
 data to every other one (`MPI_Alltoall`, and `MPI_Alltoallv` for pieces of
 different sizes). The cost of a message of \f$ b \f$ bytes is taken as
 \f$ \alpha + \beta b \f$, \f$ \alpha \f$ the latency and \f$ \beta \f$ the
 time per byte, and the cost of an algorithm is its local work, its number of
 messages and its volume of communication, all per process, i.e., along its
 slowest process.

 <table>
 <caption>The symbols of the headers of the area</caption>
 <tr><th>Symbol</th><th>Meaning</th></tr>
 <tr><td>\f$ P \f$</td>
     <td>the processes of the communicator <tt>comm</tt></td></tr>
 <tr><td>\f$ n_i \f$, \f$ n \f$</td>
     <td>the items of the process \f$ i \f$, and \f$ n = \sum_i n_i \f$ the
         items of all</td></tr>
 <tr><td>\f$ \alpha \f$, \f$ \beta \f$</td>
     <td>the latency of a message and the time per byte</td></tr>
 <tr><td>\f$ s_1 , \ldots , s_{P - 1} \f$</td>
     <td>the splitters of the sort, which cut the keys into \f$ P \f$
         intervals, one per process</td></tr>
 <tr><td>\f$ w \f$</td>
     <td>the items of a process between two of its samples,
         \f$ n_i / P \f$</td></tr>
 </table>

 A sort of the items spread over the processes leaves in the process of rank
 \f$ j \f$ a sorted vector, whose items are not smaller than those of the
 ranks before \f$ j \f$, so that the vectors in the order of the ranks are the
 sorted items of all. Every distributed sort moves most items to another
 process, since a process holds, before the sort, items of every interval:
 the volume of communication is at least about \f$ n / P \f$ per process, and
 the aim is to send each item once, in one exchange, with parts of about
 \f$ n / P \f$ items at the end.

 \section theory_distributed_sort Sorting by regular sampling

 The sort of Shi and Schaeffer (`sort()` of `DistributedSort.h`) sorts the
 items of each process, takes from them \f$ P - 1 \f$ samples at the ranks
 \f$ \lfloor ( j + 1 ) n_i / P \rfloor \f$, i.e., one every \f$ w \f$ items,
 gathers all the \f$ P ( P - 1 ) \f$ samples in every process and sorts them,
 and takes as splitters every \f$ ( P - 1 ) \f$-th of them. Each process then
 cuts its sorted items at the splitters by binary searches, sends to the
 process \f$ j \f$ its items in \f$ ( s_j , s_{j+1} ] \f$, and merges the
 \f$ P \f$ sorted pieces that it receives. Since every process cuts at the
 same splitters, the items received by the process \f$ j \f$ are exactly those
 of all the processes in its interval, and their merge is sorted; hence the
 vectors in the order of the ranks are the sorted items.

 <em>Lemma 1 (regular sampling).</em> With \f$ n / P \f$ items in each process
 and distinct keys, each process receives
 \f[
   \text{fewer than } 2 n / P \text{ items.} \tag{1}
 \f]

 Indeed, the interval of a process holds \f$ P - 1 \f$ samples, those between
 two consecutive splitters, and let \f$ t_i \f$ be those of the process
 \f$ i \f$, so that \f$ \sum_i t_i \le P - 1 \f$. The items of the process
 \f$ i \f$ in the interval lie between its sample before the first of its
 \f$ t_i \f$ samples and its sample after the last one, i.e., in at most
 \f$ t_i + 1 \f$ of its gaps of \f$ w = n / P^2 \f$ items; hence the interval
 holds at most
 \f$ \sum_i ( t_i + 1 ) w \le ( 2 P - 1 ) n / P^2 < 2 n / P \f$ items, which
 is (1). The bound is tight up to a constant, and in practice the parts are
 far closer to \f$ n / P \f$. With repeated keys (1) may fail, since all the
 items of a key go to one interval; `sort()` therefore cuts the items equal to
 a run of \f$ k \f$ equal splitters into \f$ k + 1 \f$ parts of the same size,
 so that a key repeated many times is spread over as many processes as it
 fills, and the merge keeps the result sorted since the parts are of equal
 items.

 The cost of the sort is the local sort, \f$ O( ( n / P ) \log( n / P ) ) \f$,
 the sort of the samples, \f$ O( P^2 \log P ) \f$, the \f$ P - 1 \f$ binary
 searches, and the merge of \f$ P \f$ pieces of \f$ O( n / P ) \f$ items in
 all, \f$ O( ( n / P ) \log P ) \f$, i.e., \f$ O( ( n / P ) \log n ) \f$ work
 per process when \f$ n / P \ge P^2 \f$. The communication is three
 collective operations: the gathering of the samples, \f$ P^2 \f$ items to
 each process; the exchange of the sizes of the pieces, one integer for each
 pair of processes; and the exchange of the pieces, each item sent once,
 which by (1) costs each process \f$ O( n / P ) \f$ items. With a direct
 all-to-all, in which each process sends \f$ P - 1 \f$ messages, the time of
 the communication is therefore
 \f[
   O( \alpha P + \beta ( n / P + P^2 ) ) , \tag{2}
 \f]
 which is \f$ O( \alpha P + \beta n / P ) \f$ when \f$ n / P \ge P^2 \f$.
 Beyond some thousands of processes the latency \f$ \alpha P \f$ and the
 \f$ P^2 \f$ samples dominate (2); the multi-level sorts of Axtmann, Bingmann,
 Sanders and Schulz then group the processes and exchange the items in
 \f$ O( \log P ) \f$ levels, each item sent once per level, which trades the
 \f$ P \f$ messages of each process for \f$ O( P^{1 / \ell} ) \f$ messages per
 level over \f$ \ell \f$ levels.

 \section theory_distributed_refs References

 \see H. Shi, J. Schaeffer, Parallel sorting by regular sampling, Journal
      of Parallel and Distributed Computing 14(4), 361-372, 1992,
      doi:10.1016/0743-7315(92)90075-X
 \see M. Axtmann, T. Bingmann, P. Sanders, C. Schulz, Practical massively
      parallel sorting, ACM Symposium on Parallelism in Algorithms and
      Architectures (SPAA), 13-23, 2015, doi:10.1145/2755573.2755595
*/
